  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Typhoon Champi kills one and injures more than 500 in Taiwan as it heads to China",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/24/typhoon-champi-kills-one-and-injures-more-than-500-in-taiwan-as-it-heads-to-china",
      "description": "<p>Super typhoon weakening but large parts of Taiwan, including Taipei, still mostly closed and flights cancelled</p><p>Typhoon Champi swept past Taiwan on Saturday killing one person, injuring more than 500, and uprooting trees and severing power as it weakened and ⁠headed north toward ⁠the ​Chinese coast.</p><p>Earlier classified as a super typhoon and one of the strongest storms in the ⁠Pacific this year, Champi was still packing gusts of about 126km per hour (78 miles per hour) near its ⁠centre, according to Taiwan’s Central Weather Administration (CWA).</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/24/typhoon-champi-kills-one-and-injures-more-than-500-in-taiwan-as-it-heads-to-china\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-24T02:22:32"
    },
    {
      "title": "Typhoon Champi kills one, injures 350 in Taiwan; 280,000 homes lose power",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/typhoon-champi-kills-one-injures-350-in-taiwan-280-000-homes-lose-power-126102400133_1.html",
      "description": "Typhoon Champi killed one person and injured hundreds in Taiwan, as well as downing trees and knocking out power for hundreds of thousands of people as the storm's strong winds and rains continued overnight Friday into Saturday morning.\nAlthough Champi did not hit Taiwan directly, the storm's span was large enough to impact most of Taiwan. The island's weather authorities said the storm was weakening Saturday morning and that by evening, the land warnings for the typhoon would be removed.\nTaiwan's Central Emergency Operations Centre reported that 350 were injured as of Friday night. The one death was a man who was washed away by waves in Taiwan's Pingtung county, the island's southernmost part.\nThe storm knocked out power for 280,000 households, according to United Daily News, a local Taiwanese newspaper, citing the island's power authorities. But power had returned to 80 per cent of households by Saturday morning.\nAcross the island, the storm downed trees and signs, some smaller .",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-24T04:24:19"
    }
